#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c2c83b5-ac91-57a3-ac6c-76f0fbbdbbe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382653} {:answer/id #uuid "28850b83-c4b0-57bb-8209-a7274e569b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382654} {:answer/id #uuid "a850083c-117b-59f3-ba15-6d5a442d2621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382655} {:answer/id #uuid "e93a9c55-8fec-550d-808f-afd03f26d3cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382656} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f5a770-68f5-5722-8f61-48518553a391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9f5a770-68f5-5722-8f61-48518553a391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382657} :answer-field/id #uuid "484e247f-cdf6-58ee-b821-37819ce89d49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16966b44-d262-59a2-bf48-8567403d396f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140045" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ea0d80-d8ed-5742-b580-acd17e924317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360848} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0d929e-1535-5edd-adea-6402afa67fbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360849} {:answer/id #uuid "46e1bdb9-47a0-58f8-83a8-1ef8d065d844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360850} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed6a480-f8b8-55c5-8817-b1751fbb6d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360851} {:answer/id #uuid "8c75cfe3-9149-5a88-becc-c45ae44dee53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d0d929e-1535-5edd-adea-6402afa67fbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360849} :answer-field/id #uuid "49cc2694-4ab7-58b9-8c75-08236df282a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c21ab48-f38e-5c23-b250-b441e717ec06" :question/math-academy-id "q-140053" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & 5 \\\\ b & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba32fd11-6da9-501e-a2d9-85de306160b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382660} {:answer/id #uuid "4feddca6-5376-5c7a-a476-c66a930662bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382661} {:answer/id #uuid "23c2a8a5-6898-5a4b-b709-8b8c7b090341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382662} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb82560-b42c-5520-8c60-98a9a34de52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382663} {:answer/id #uuid "793349a3-14e1-5bde-ae92-cb88d946180d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23c2a8a5-6898-5a4b-b709-8b8c7b090341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382662} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e607a58-c096-5640-8687-a2892d9455d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4b19937-90d1-5717-b7a0-7b53ad9d3dcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-37633" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}BP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=7,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-5)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "689eac4c-f400-5205-9edc-85d32a07078d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360875} {:answer/id #uuid "8028f3d3-4f5f-5c35-bab0-116412fa92bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360876} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e9745a-c762-5395-8a28-37815142b6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360877} {:answer/id #uuid "d470eb6a-e864-5067-8e28-3088088a34f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360878} {:answer/id #uuid "853eec6a-d8ff-5b36-922a-3f60256d0bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8028f3d3-4f5f-5c35-bab0-116412fa92bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360876} :answer-field/id #uuid "e846d85d-195f-5ac9-ba6f-bba36859de02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ba37aab-505d-5609-a7ff-5dc6d88539eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-80455" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-12) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9655b0d7-ff30-5b71-8896-d548cc1640c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360896} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa3f5e6-34ca-5527-bf94-15d4078be3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360897} {:answer/id #uuid "e33a854f-8165-5bd7-a7a9-f146d4dee9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360898} {:answer/id #uuid "3acc888d-701f-54d6-8ee4-df0d0249b48c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360899} {:answer/id #uuid "259e4ebc-b9cb-5c55-be35-4c02a1a58142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3acc888d-701f-54d6-8ee4-df0d0249b48c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360899} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fee65d7-f331-5c0a-a85c-18a138015b6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7be4462e-3d0b-5745-b83e-db8e5d74f57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80493" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
